WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.493
[DRUMS PLAYING]

00:00:12.990 --> 00:00:17.335
It's really cool that MIT flew
out an entire 25 piece big band

00:00:17.335 --> 00:00:18.460
all the way to Puerto Rico.

00:00:18.460 --> 00:00:21.722
[DRUMS PLAYING]

00:00:24.530 --> 00:00:26.630
When I was a freshman
first joining the ensemble,

00:00:26.630 --> 00:00:28.588
it's not something I
would ever have imagined--

00:00:28.588 --> 00:00:30.690
going on tour with Miguel
Zenón in Puerto Rico.

00:00:30.690 --> 00:00:34.141
[SAXOPHONE PLAYING]

00:00:42.029 --> 00:00:45.480
[DRUMS PLAYING]

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:47.970
[APPLAUSE]

00:00:47.970 --> 00:00:50.650
Now that you saw the
demonstration that they had,

00:00:50.650 --> 00:00:52.840
I think [SPEAKING SPANISH].

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:57.130
He wanted to see if
there were any volunteers

00:00:57.130 --> 00:00:58.165
to come up and dance.

00:00:58.165 --> 00:01:01.438
[AUDIENCE CHEERING]

00:01:03.430 --> 00:01:06.916
[DRUMS PLAYING]

00:01:22.370 --> 00:01:24.230
My parents are Nigerian.

00:01:24.230 --> 00:01:28.070
They listen to a lot of
traditional African music.

00:01:28.070 --> 00:01:30.860
And from a rhythmic
standpoint, it's very similar.

00:01:30.860 --> 00:01:34.248
[JAZZ MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:37.152 --> 00:01:40.540
[SPEAKING SPANISH]

00:01:41.510 --> 00:01:44.558
A big part of MIT's
vision is to take

00:01:44.558 --> 00:01:46.850
all the skills and the
knowledge that the students have

00:01:46.850 --> 00:01:50.270
acquired, and to go forth
and spread it, and try

00:01:50.270 --> 00:01:52.150
to use those skills for good.

00:01:52.150 --> 00:01:54.590
And I really think that
this trip is very much

00:01:54.590 --> 00:01:58.118
an extension of MIT's
dedication to trying

00:01:58.118 --> 00:01:59.410
to change the world positively.

00:01:59.410 --> 00:02:02.889
[JAZZ MUSIC PLAYING]

00:02:12.830 --> 00:02:14.230
My name is Peter.

00:02:14.230 --> 00:02:16.700
I'm from Massachusetts.

00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:18.800
I go to the Massachusetts
Institute of Technology.

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:20.840
Today, we've been
talking to middle school

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:21.980
and high schoolers.

00:02:21.980 --> 00:02:23.450
And my goal throughout
this process

00:02:23.450 --> 00:02:25.520
is to inspire them to
begin thinking about ways

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:28.760
that they can directly
impact their own communities.

00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:31.710
This area was very affected by
Hurricane Maria with flooding

00:02:31.710 --> 00:02:35.000
of a few feet of water that
stayed inside the houses

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:36.830
for 16 days.

00:02:36.830 --> 00:02:39.380
And they had no power
for around four months.

00:02:39.380 --> 00:02:41.200
[JAZZ MUSIC PLAYING]

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:44.710
I've seen a remarkable
example of the resiliency

00:02:44.710 --> 00:02:46.750
of the human spirit,
and people's ability

00:02:46.750 --> 00:02:49.810
to recover in any situation.

00:02:49.810 --> 00:02:52.360
Close to 300 schools
have been closed

00:02:52.360 --> 00:02:53.680
as a result of the storm.

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:57.070
And the people are now moving
to reclaim these properties,

00:02:57.070 --> 00:03:00.340
and convert them into something
else like a multicultural arts

00:03:00.340 --> 00:03:03.250
center that will give students
and artists in the community

00:03:03.250 --> 00:03:06.040
a place to rehearse, or a place
to perform, or just a place

00:03:06.040 --> 00:03:08.300
to gather and share their art.

00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:11.263
In the school we just visited,
there is no electricity.

00:03:11.263 --> 00:03:13.180
And so right now, they're
trying to figure out

00:03:13.180 --> 00:03:16.330
how can they take solar
panels and batteries

00:03:16.330 --> 00:03:20.150
and supply the power required
to run that facility.

00:03:20.150 --> 00:03:23.170
I'm extremely grateful for
you guys being here this week.

00:03:23.170 --> 00:03:25.330
I'm sure you know how
much this means to me,

00:03:25.330 --> 00:03:27.350
and how long we've worked
to make this happen.

00:03:27.350 --> 00:03:29.920
This has exceeded
all my expectations.

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:32.823
And hopefully, you got
something really good from it.

00:03:32.823 --> 00:03:34.240
I can assure you
that everyone you

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:36.940
met this week and everyone
who heard you play and heard

00:03:36.940 --> 00:03:39.430
you speak got a lot from
it and is going to make

00:03:39.430 --> 00:03:40.730
a difference in their life.

00:03:40.730 --> 00:03:42.290
So thank you so
much for being here.

00:03:42.290 --> 00:03:43.582
I really, really appreciate it.

00:03:43.582 --> 00:03:46.902
[PIANO MUSIC PLAYING]

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:55.600
I get so much joy out of seeing
this kind of thing happen.

00:03:55.600 --> 00:03:57.670
It's the greatest thing
that could happen.

00:03:57.670 --> 00:04:00.270
And it's the greatest reward
that music could give me.

00:04:00.270 --> 00:04:02.370
[FESTIVE MUSIC PLAYING]

00:04:39.270 --> 00:04:41.370
[AUDIENCE CHEERING]

00:04:41.920 --> 00:04:43.170
[AUDIENCE SHOUTING IN SPANISH]

00:04:43.170 --> 00:04:46.520
[AUDIENCE CHEERING]

